course: language
lesson: 16
title: Formal and informal: a mate versus the head teacher
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Everyone has at least two ways of talking: informal (relaxed and chatty, for mates and family) and formal (careful and polite, for teachers, strangers and official letters); the style you choose is called your register.
explain: Picture Ruby: to her mate she texts "omg skool was SO mid today 💀", and ten minutes later she emails the head: "Dear Mrs Patel, I think I left my coat in the hall. Could you let me know if anyone has found it? Thank you, Ruby."
explain: Formal doesn't mean long fancy words; it means clear, polite and calm, with full words, a greeting like "Dear" or "Hello", and a "thank you".
explain: Rudeness rarely works: "Your park is trash and the swings are broke" gets ignored, but "I am writing about the broken swings in Oak Park; could you please arrange a repair?" gets read.
explain: Informal talk is full of local warmth: a Welsh nan wants a cwtch (a hug), a West Country gran calls everyone "my lover", a London lad greets his mate with "Wagwan, fam!"; switching between styles is a skill, not being fake.
fact: Informal language is relaxed and chatty; formal language is careful and polite, used for teachers, strangers and official writing.
fact: A register is the style of language you choose for a situation.
fact: In current slang "mid" means average, not great, and the skull emoji 💀 means "I'm dead", that is, it's so funny or so bad.
fact: Formal writing means clear and polite, not complicated; full words, a greeting and "thank you" are usually enough.
fact: Wagwan means what's going on, or hello; it came from Jamaican Creole into the speech of young Londoners; "fam" means a close friend.
fact: A good complaint letter states the problem, gives the facts and asks politely; people are more likely to help when you are polite.
fact: "Residence" is a formal word for home; answering the phone with a polite greeting is safer when the caller might be a stranger.
fact: In Welsh English a cwtch is a hug or cuddle (from Welsh), and in South Wales "tamping" means really annoyed.
fact: School reports use formal words: "conscientious" means careful and hard-working, and "contributes enthusiastically" means joins in with energy.
fact: Whether children should say please to voice assistants like Alexa is argued about; some parents want it for good habits, others say it doesn't matter.
fact: Filler words like um, like and you know are fine in casual talk; in formal talk, clear examples sound more confident.
fact: In the West Country "my lover" is a friendly greeting to anyone, like "love" or "mate", and "proper job" means well done or great.
exercise: Ruby texts her mate "skool was SO mid". What does "mid" mean? | Average, not great, nothing special about it at all | Halfway through the school day, at the middle | Brilliant, the best day of the whole week | Very short, over before lunch started
exercise: Which is the right way for Ruby to start her email to the head teacher? | Dear Mrs Patel, | Yo Mrs P, what's good, hope ur well innit | Oi, head teacher, about my coat right | Mrs Patel lol quick question about my coat
exercise: What is a register? | The style of language you choose for a situation or person | A list of names a teacher reads out each morning | A machine in a shop that takes your money | The loudness of your voice when you are cross
exercise: Ben writes "Hey Mr Jones, can u give me my book back thx". What would be better? | Hello Mr Jones, could you please give me my book back? Thank you. | Hey Mr Jones, can u give my book back thx, cheers mate | Mr Jones, I demand that my book be returned to me today | Hello Mr Jones, I hereby request the restitution of my book
exercise: Does formal writing need long fancy words? | No, it needs to be clear, calm and polite, with full words | Yes, the longer the words, the more formal the writing becomes | Yes, at least one word in every sentence must be from Latin | No, it must use only very short words of one syllable each
exercise: A South London boy says "Wagwan, fam!" to his friend. What does he mean? | Hello, what's going on, my close mate? | Wake up, family, it's time for school | Wait, we're going on a family trip | Where are you going, my family?
exercise: He then says "Good morning, Sir" to his teacher. What is he doing? | Switching registers to suit the person, a real language skill | Being fake and two-faced with his teacher | Forgetting how he normally talks at home | Speaking a completely different language
exercise: Grace writes to the council "Your park is trash and the swings are broke." What will probably help more? | A calm letter with facts and a polite request | An even ruder letter, so they know she's serious | A letter in capital letters to show she is angry | No letter at all, because councils never read them
exercise: Tom's grandma answers the phone "Hello, Thompson residence." What does "residence" mean? | Home | A hotel where you pay to stay the night | A doctor's waiting room or surgery | The person who answers the phone
exercise: Erin's Welsh nan says "Give me a cwtch, I'm tamping you're late!" What does she want? | A hug, though she's cross you're late | A cup of tea, because she's tired of waiting | A kitchen towel to wipe the table | A taxi, because she's going to be late
exercise: Dev's report says he is "conscientious". Is that good? | Yes, it means careful and hard-working, which is real praise | No, it means he talks too much in class and disturbs others | No, it means he is often late and forgets his homework | It means nothing, reports just use random long words
exercise: Sienna's brother says you don't need to say please to Alexa. What is true? | It's argued; some parents want it for good habits | It's a law that you must always say please to machines | Machines get angry and stop working if you are rude | Alexa only answers if you say please three times
exercise: Mum's friend says "Like, yeah, I'm, like, really good at stuff" in a job interview. What would sound better? | A clear example of something he really did well | Saying "like" even more, to sound relaxed and friendly | Using only one-word answers so he can't go wrong | Telling a long joke so the interviewer likes him
exercise: Lena's Cornish gran says "Alright my lover? Proper job!" What is she doing? | Greeting her warmly and saying well done | Saying something romantic to Lena's friend | Telling her off for doing a bad job | Asking if she has a proper job yet
exercise: In a formal letter, what usually goes at the end? | Thank you, and your name | lol, and a smiley face with a wink | gtg, see ya, and three kisses | Nothing at all, letters just stop
test: Your mate goes "talking posh to teachers is well fake, bruv." What's the best reply? | Choosing a style for the person is a skill, not fakeness | He's right, you should talk to the head exactly like a mate | He's right, and teachers prefer slang because it is shorter | Talking politely is only needed if you are in trouble
test: Your Gen Z cousin texts "that test was mid 💀". How is she feeling about the test? | It was not great, kind of bad, and she's being dramatic about it | She did brilliantly and is very proud of it | The test was cancelled halfway through the lesson | She is really ill and has to go home now
test: What would happen if you wrote a job letter full of text speak like "u" and "thx"? | It might look careless, so they may not take you seriously | Nothing at all, every company prefers text speak these days | They would offer you the job straight away for being modern | The letter would be sent back because computers can't read it
test: A boomer neighbour says "kids today can't write a proper letter." Your sister writes to the council about the swings. Which opening is best? | I am writing about the broken swings in Oak Park. | Oi, sort them swings out, they're well dead, yeah? | To whom it may concern, ur swings r broke, fix them | Hello council, the swings, you know what I mean, ok?
test: A Welsh lad says "Mam's tamping, I'm off home now in a minute." ("Mam" is mum.) How does his mum feel? | Very cross and annoyed with him | Very happy and calm | Very sleepy and tired | Very hungry and thirsty
test: Your American friend's teacher writes "Sloane is a conscientious student." Her friend says it sounds like an insult. Is it? | No, it means careful and hard-working, so it's a compliment | Yes, it means she never listens in lessons and chats | Yes, it means she copies other pupils' homework | No, it means she is the tallest in her class
test: A Liverpool kid answers his mum's phone "Wha'?" ("Wha'" is a quick "what?") and it's the head teacher. What should he have said? | Hello, who's calling, please? | Wha', who is it, la, I'm busy | What do you want, I'm watching telly | Nothing, he should have just hung up
test: In a cartoon, a knight speaks to his best mate like a king's speech: "Prithee, good sir, pass the crisps." What's funny about it? | He uses a very formal style for a chatty moment | Knights were not allowed to eat crisps at all | Prithee is a word for crisps in old English | Nothing, everyone talks to their mates like that
test: Gran from Bristol says "Cheers, my lover" to the postman. Your friend from London is shocked. What's the truth? | It's a friendly West Country greeting to anyone | Gran is in love with the postman and is telling him | It's a rude thing to say to a postman in Bristol | It means she wants the postman to go away
test: An interviewer asks "What are you good at?" Which answer sounds most confident? | I'm good at organising; I ran the school fair. | Like, um, yeah, I'm like good at loads of stuff, innit. | Stuff, really, all kinds of stuff, you know, things. | I don't know, maybe everything, or maybe nothing.
test: Your little brother says "Alexa, weather, now!" and your mum says "Manners!" What is true? | People disagree about manners with machines; with people, always be polite | It's illegal to speak to a machine without saying please | Alexa will delete his account if he doesn't say please | Machines feel hurt and cry when people don't say please
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises formal language? | Language that is careful, clear and courteous, appropriate to official purposes | Language that employs the greatest possible number of lengthy words in each sentence | Language reserved exclusively for persons of advanced years | Language composed principally of abbreviations and symbols
exam: Identify the course of action most likely to secure the repair of defective equipment in a public park. | Correspondence stating the facts and requesting repair courteously | Correspondence employing abusive language so as to convey urgency | The abstention from any communication with the authority concerned | The composition of a letter entirely in capital letters
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the alteration of style between conversation with friends and correspondence with a head teacher. | It constitutes a recognised linguistic competence | It constitutes evidence of dishonesty on the part of the writer | It is prohibited by the regulations of most schools | It indicates an inability to speak the language properly
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the word "conscientious" as employed in a school report. | Careful and diligent | Inclined to excessive talking during lessons | Habitually late in the submission of homework | Of unusually great height compared with peers
